  "live_records": {
    "q-40312": {
      "dom_id": "step-q40312",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14132825/assets/c24eb387b430ae40e7c5ec0747eb8570df21ebbe4535fd1305676219ad5c83f1.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the front one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14132825/assets/d00feacd712c1ca185a8975e8b6343f9937f254631614242003f1d76c1427ffe.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type II region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(y,z)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+y-z\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14132825/assets/59b7304dcc07ac38c093b7d8d29d4b4079f192cd9fd0385db9f1981e4640eceb.png)\n\nHence, the type II representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2+y-z\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2+y-z}dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2+y-z}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2+y-z)-0\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2+y-z\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(2+y)z-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=0}^{z=2}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}((2+y)(2)-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2})-0\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2+2y\\,dy \\\\  & =[2y+{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =(2(3)+{3}^{2})-0 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
